Q: In C# what is the best way to detect that an optional parameter wasn&#39;t specified? A: <p>In case of an optional method there is not technically feasible way to determine whether a parameter has been passed, because whatever the default value is, the caller might have passed that specific default value and there is no way inside the function to differentiate between a default value being actively passed, or the value being default because of not being passed. However, there are conventional ways to &quot;know&quot; it.</p>
<p>For example, you could state that a value should never ever be passed to that method for that parameter, agree with the team to reject any PRs that violate this rule and then, by convention, the default value &quot;means&quot; that there was no value passed.</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<pre><code>public void ExampleMethod(int required, int myStrictPositive = 0)
{
    //...
}
</code></pre>
<p>Even though it is technically possible to pass 0 as <code>myStrictPositive</code>, rejecting or even auto-rejecting any PRs that would possibly result in this would compel members of the team to modify codes like</p>
<pre><code>obj.ExampleMethod(x, y);
</code></pre>
<p>to</p>
<pre><code>if (y == 0) {
    ExampleMethod(x);
} else {
    ExampleMethod(x, y);
}
</code></pre>
<p>This overcomplicates the code and makes it hard to read, which probably defeats the purpose. However, you can do method overloading:</p>
<pre><code>public void ExampleMethod(int required)
{
    //myStrictPositive was not passed
}

public void ExampleMethod(int required, int myStrictPositive = 0)
{
    //myStrictPositive was passed
}
</code></pre>
<p>Technically you do not use an optional parameter with the format of <code>T p = v</code>, but method overloading, but essentially <code>myStrictPositive</code> is optional.</p>
<p>EDIT</p>
<p>Greg Burghardt raised an important doubt in the comment-section, that is, can we rely on an optional parameter overload like I described above? I have conducted an experiment to confirm, namely:</p>
<pre><code>using System;

public class Program
{
    public void ExampleMethod(int required)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(&quot;strict positive was not passed&quot;);
    }

    public void ExampleMethod(int required, int myStrictPositive = 0)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(&quot;strict positive was passed as &quot; + myStrictPositive);
    }   
    public static void Main()
    {
        Program program = new Program();
        program.ExampleMethod(123, 0);
        program.ExampleMethod(123);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>and the output was</p>
<pre><code>strict positive was passed as 0
strict positive was not passed
</code></pre>
<p>as expected, tested using .NET 10 at <a href="https://dotnetfiddle.net/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://dotnetfiddle.net/</a> .</p>
